-- Q2 [Muscle Fiber Types] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A sprinter''s leg muscles rely heavily on fast glycolytic (white) fibers to make rapid, powerful contractions during a sprint. These fibers most likely generate \(\text{ATP}\) primarily through which process?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aerobic respiration in abundant mitochondria"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oxidative metabolism using stored myoglobin oxygen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis of stored glycogen"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Sustained aerobic glucose breakdown resisting fatigue"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because fast glycolytic (FG) fibers primarily use anaerobic glycolysis of their large glycogen stores to generate ATP quickly for powerful, short contractions. The textbook states that "FG fibers primarily use anaerobic glycolysis as their ATP source" and that "they have a large diameter and possess high amounts of glycogen, which is used in glycolysis to generate ATP quickly to produce high levels of tension." Because they rely on anaerobic glycolysis rather than aerobic metabolism, these fibers fatigue quickly and can only be used for short periods, which fits the rapid, powerful demand of a sprint. (Choice A) is incorrect because abundant mitochondria and aerobic respiration characterize oxidative fibers, not glycolytic fibers; the text notes FG fibers "do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria." (Choice B) is incorrect because significant myoglobin and its stored oxygen belong to slow oxidative (red) fibers, whereas FG fibers "do not possess... significant amounts of myoglobin and therefore have a white color." (Choice C) is correct: FG fibers depend on anaerobic glycolysis of stored glycogen to rapidly supply ATP for forceful, short-duration contractions. (Choice D) is incorrect because sustained, fatigue-resistant aerobic ATP production is the hallmark of slow oxidative fibers that "sustain muscle activity without fatiguing for long periods," the opposite of fast glycolytic fibers that "fatigue quickly." This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the primary ATP-generating pathway of fast glycolytic muscle fibers.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
