-- Q3 [Muscle Fiber Types] easy skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A deep muscle of the lower back holds the spine upright throughout an eight-hour workday, contracting continuously without fatiguing. This muscle is most likely dominated by which type of skeletal muscle fiber?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Fast glycolytic fibers"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Slow oxidative fibers"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fast oxidative fibers"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cardiac muscle fibers"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because slow oxidative fibers sustain low-tension contractions for long periods without fatiguing, which is exactly what a postural muscle requires. Slow oxidative (SO) fibers use aerobic respiration, contain many mitochondria, are richly supplied with capillaries, and store \(\text{O}_2\) in myoglobin, allowing them to produce large quantities of \(\text{ATP}\) and sustain muscle activity without fatiguing for long periods of time. The textbook states directly that these features make SO fibers "useful in maintaining posture, producing isometric contractions, stabilizing bones and joints" and that "postural muscles have a large number of SO fibers and relatively few FO and FG fibers, to keep the back straight." (Choice A) is wrong: fast glycolytic fibers primarily use anaerobic glycolysis, "fatigue quickly, permitting them to only be used for short periods," and produce rapid, forceful movements rather than sustained posture. (Choice B) is correct: SO fibers'' aerobic metabolism, abundant mitochondria, and myoglobin \(\text{O}_2\) reserve let them resist fatigue across hours of continuous low-force contraction, the defining demand of a postural muscle. (Choice C) is wrong: fast oxidative fibers are fatigue-resistant but are used for movements such as walking that require more energy than postural control, and because they may switch to anaerobic glycolysis they fatigue more quickly than SO fibers. (Choice D) is wrong: cardiac muscle fibers are found only in the heart wall, not in skeletal postural muscles, so they are not a type of skeletal fiber that holds the spine upright. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the metabolic and fatigue properties of each fiber type to a specific functional demand.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
