-- Q4 [Muscle Fiber Types] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A histologist comparing two skeletal muscle fibers finds that one is densely packed with mitochondria while the other contains very few. The mitochondria-rich fiber is most directly suited to which capacity?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Sustaining ATP production through aerobic metabolism"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Generating ATP rapidly through anaerobic glycolysis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Producing high tension through faster cross-bridge cycling"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Storing reserve \\(\\text{O}_2\\) within the sarcoplasm itself"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because aerobic metabolism occurs inside mitochondria, so a fiber packed with mitochondria is built to make ATP aerobically for sustained, fatigue-resistant contraction. The textbook states that oxidative fibers contain many more mitochondria than glycolytic fibers "because aerobic metabolism, which uses oxygen (O2) in the metabolic pathway, occurs in the mitochondria" (lines 17821-17822). Slow oxidative fibers possess a large number of mitochondria and can contract for longer periods because of the large amount of ATP they produce aerobically, allowing them to sustain activity without fatiguing (lines 17822-17829). This is exactly why abundant mitochondria mark a fiber for endurance rather than for brief, powerful bursts. (Choice A) is correct: the high mitochondrial density reflects reliance on oxidative (aerobic) ATP production, which yields more ATP per cycle and resists fatigue (lines 17813-17819, 17821-17822). (Choice B) reverses the relationship, because anaerobic glycolysis is the pathway of fast glycolytic fibers, which "do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria"; glycolysis proceeds in the cytoplasm, not the mitochondria (lines 17845-17847). (Choice C) names an adjacent property that is unrelated to mitochondria, since contraction speed and rapid cross-bridge cycling depend on how quickly myosin ATPase hydrolyzes ATP, with fast fibers hydrolyzing ATP about twice as quickly as slow fibers (lines 17809-17811). (Choice D) confuses organelles, because intracellular \(\text{O}_2\) storage is the role of myoglobin held in the sarcoplasm, not of mitochondria, which consume \(\text{O}_2\) rather than store it (lines 17826-17827, 17872-17873). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that mitochondria are the site of aerobic (oxidative) ATP production and link their abundance to sustained, fatigue-resistant contraction.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
