-- Q5 [Muscle Fiber Types] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'The deep paraspinal muscles must hold the spine erect for hours at a time without tiring, even though they generate only modest tension. The fiber type expected to predominate in these postural muscles most likely contracts in which manner?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Quickly and forcefully using anaerobic glycolysis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Quickly and aerobically with little myoglobin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Slowly and aerobically while resisting fatigue"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Slowly and anaerobically while resisting fatigue"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because postural muscles, whose primary function is sustained low-tension contraction, predominantly contain slow oxidative (SO) fibers that contract slowly, generate \(\text{ATP}\) aerobically, and resist fatigue for long periods. The textbook states that the predominant fiber type in a muscle is determined by its primary function, and that "Postural muscles have a large number of SO fibers and relatively few FO and FG fibers, to keep the back straight." SO fibers contract slowly, use aerobic respiration, possess abundant mitochondria and myoglobin, and "can function for long periods without fatiguing," which "makes them useful in maintaining posture." Because holding the spine erect requires only modest, sustained tension rather than rapid powerful bursts, the fatigue-resistant SO fiber is the functional match. (Choice A) describes fast glycolytic (FG) fibers, which "use anaerobic glycolysis" to "produce rapid, forceful contractions" but "fatigue quickly," making them suited to resistance/power movements, not sustained posture. (Choice B) describes fast oxidative (FO) fibers, which contract quickly, are aerobic, but "do not possess significant myoglobin"; they serve intermediate movements such as walking, not low-tension postural maintenance. (Choice C) is correct: SO fibers contract slowly using aerobic (oxidative) metabolism and resist fatigue, the exact profile favored when a muscle''s primary function is sustaining posture. (Choice D) reverses the metabolic pathway of slow fibers; slow fibers are oxidative (aerobic), not anaerobic, so although fatigue resistance is correctly paired with slow contraction, the "anaerobically" descriptor contradicts the defining oxidative metabolism of SO fibers. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that a muscle''s primary function sets its fiber-type proportions to predict the metabolic and contractile profile of a specific postural muscle.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
