-- Q6 [Muscle Fiber Types] hard skill4 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A physiologist profiles three skeletal muscle fiber types from a leg muscle and records the following:

| Fiber | Myoglobin | Mitochondria | Time to fatigue |
|-------|-----------|--------------|-----------------|
| W | High | Many | Long |
| X | Low | Many | Intermediate |
| Y | Low | Few | Short |

A sprinter relies on explosive, short-duration, high-tension contractions to drive a 100-meter dash. Based on the data, which fiber most directly powers this effort?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Fiber W, because abundant myoglobin sustains aerobic output"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fiber X, because intermediate fibers resist fatigue during sprints"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fiber W, because high mitochondrial density maximizes power"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fiber Y, because anaerobic glycolysis yields rapid, forceful contraction"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the low-myoglobin, few-mitochondria, quick-to-fatigue profile of fiber Y identifies a fast glycolytic fiber, the type that relies on anaerobic glycolysis to drive rapid, forceful contractions. The textbook states that fast glycolytic (FG) fibers "primarily use anaerobic glycolysis as their ATP source," possess high glycogen "used in glycolysis to generate ATP quickly to produce high levels of tension," and "do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria or significant amounts of myoglobin" (lines 17845-17848). Because they generate ATP quickly but not efficiently, "these fibers fatigue quickly, permitting them to only be used for short periods," and are "used to produce rapid, forceful contractions to make quick, powerful movements" (lines 17848-17850) — precisely the demand of an explosive sprint, which the text explicitly contrasts against the intermediate FO role (line 17841). Fiber Y''s data signature (low myoglobin, few mitochondria, short time to fatigue) is the unique fingerprint of this fiber. (Choice A) is wrong: fiber W''s abundant myoglobin and many mitochondria mark it as a slow oxidative fiber, which "do not produce a large amount of tension" and "are not used for powerful, fast movements that require high amounts of energy and rapid cross-bridge cycling" (lines 17824, 17833-17834), so aerobic output cannot supply a maximal burst. (Choice B) is wrong: fiber X (low myoglobin, many mitochondria, intermediate fatigue) is a fast oxidative fiber suited to sustained movements like walking, "less energy than an explosive movement, such as sprinting" (lines 17840-17841); it is the runner-up but not the most direct power source for an explosive sprint. (Choice C) is wrong: it again selects the slow oxidative fiber W, and high mitochondrial density supports endurance, not peak power — "aerobic metabolism... occurs in the mitochondria" and yields fatigue resistance, not the rapid high-tension output of glycolytic fibers (lines 17821, 17818-17819). (Choice D) is correct: only the fast glycolytic profile couples anaerobic glycolysis to the quick, high-tension, short-duration contraction an explosive sprint requires. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated fiber properties to infer the underlying metabolic mechanism and predict which fiber meets the contractile demand.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
