-- Q7 [Muscle Fiber Types] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A histochemical analysis of a skeletal muscle biopsy identifies fibers that stain strongly for mitochondrial oxidative enzymes and resist fatigue during sustained activity, yet appear notably paler than the deep-red postural fibers in the same sample. The fibers that best match this profile most likely correspond to which muscle fiber type?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Fast oxidative (intermediate) fibers"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Slow oxidative fibers"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fast glycolytic fibers"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Smooth muscle fibers"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because fast oxidative (intermediate) fibers produce \(\text{ATP}\) aerobically with abundant mitochondria and resist fatigue, yet lack significant myoglobin, which makes them paler than the red slow oxidative fibers. The textbook states that FO fibers "are oxidative because they produce ATP aerobically, possess high amounts of mitochondria, and do not fatigue quickly. However, FO fibers do not possess significant myoglobin, giving them a lighter color than the red SO fibers" (lines 17838-17840). The described profile chains two features—strong oxidative-enzyme staining plus fatigue resistance (an aerobic signature) together with a pale color (low myoglobin)—and only the intermediate FO fiber satisfies both. (Choice A) is correct: it is the only fiber type that is genuinely oxidative and fatigue-resistant while still lacking the myoglobin that would deepen its color, exactly the intermediate phenotype between SO and FG fibers. (Choice B) is wrong because slow oxidative fibers also possess high mitochondrial content but additionally "possess myoglobin... and gives SO fibers their red color" (lines 17826-17827); their deep-red appearance contradicts the paler staining described, so SO fibers are the deep-red postural fibers the biopsy contrasts against, not the pale ones. (Choice C) is wrong because fast glycolytic fibers "do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria or significant amounts of myoglobin" (lines 17847-17848); they would indeed be pale, but they would not stain strongly for mitochondrial oxidative enzymes or resist fatigue, since they "fatigue quickly" (line 17849). (Choice D) is wrong because smooth muscle is not one of the three skeletal muscle fiber types classified by contraction speed and metabolism—"there are three main types of skeletal muscle fibers" (line 17802)—and the classification scheme described here applies to skeletal fibers. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining metabolic and myoglobin criteria for each fiber type to an unfamiliar histochemical profile and infer the matching fiber type.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
