-- Q8 [Muscle Fiber Types] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Fiber Types', 'A physiologist isolates two skeletal muscle fibers that both generate \(\text{ATP}\) almost entirely through aerobic respiration and contain comparable numbers of mitochondria, yet one fiber shortens roughly twice as fast as the other during a twitch. The faster-shortening fiber most likely owes its greater contraction speed to which property?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A greater number of mitochondria supplying \\(\\text{ATP}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A higher rate of \\(\\text{ATP}\\) hydrolysis by myosin ATPase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A greater reliance on anaerobic glycolysis for \\(\\text{ATP}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A higher myoglobin content storing \\(\\text{O}_2\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the speed of contraction is set by how quickly myosin''s ATPase hydrolyzes \(\text{ATP}\) to drive cross-bridge cycling, not by the fiber''s metabolic pathway. The textbook states that "the speed of contraction is dependent on how quickly myosin''s ATPase hydrolyzes ATP to produce cross-bridge action" and that "fast fibers hydrolyze ATP approximately twice as quickly as slow fibers, resulting in much quicker cross-bridge cycling" (lines 17809-17811). Because both isolated fibers are oxidative and similarly endowed with mitochondria, the metabolic route of \(\text{ATP}\) supply is held constant; the only property that explains a roughly twofold difference in shortening speed is the intrinsic rate of myosin ATPase activity. The text confirms these are two independent criteria, since "the primary metabolic pathway used by a muscle fiber determines whether the fiber is classified as oxidative or glycolytic" (lines 17811-17813), a separate axis from speed. The faster fiber here is a fast oxidative (FO) fiber, which has "fast contractions" yet "produce[s] ATP aerobically" (lines 17804, 17838). (Choice A) is incorrect because mitochondrial number governs how much \(\text{ATP}\) can be produced aerobically and thus fatigue resistance, not the rate of cross-bridge cycling; the stem also fixes mitochondrial number as comparable between the two fibers, so it cannot be the distinguishing variable. (Choice B) is correct: a higher myosin ATPase hydrolysis rate produces faster cross-bridge cycling and therefore faster shortening, the textbook''s stated determinant of contraction speed. (Choice C) is incorrect because reliance on anaerobic glycolysis defines a glycolytic rather than oxidative fiber and affects fatigue rate and \(\text{ATP}\) yield per cycle, not contraction speed; moreover the stem specifies both fibers are aerobic, so neither uses glycolysis as its primary source. (Choice D) is incorrect because myoglobin stores \(\text{O}_2\) within the fiber to support sustained aerobic \(\text{ATP}\) production and gives slow oxidative fibers their red color, a feature tied to endurance and \(\text{O}_2\) reserve rather than to how fast myosin cycles. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that contraction speed tracks myosin ATPase rate to a controlled comparison in which metabolic supply is held constant.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
