-- Q10 [Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics', 'A person lifts a feather and then a heavy dumbbell using the same biceps brachii muscle, generating far greater force for the dumbbell. Because each muscle fiber contracts in an all-or-none fashion, the increased force is most directly produced by which change in the muscle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A larger action potential within each active fiber"}, {"label": "B", "text": "An increase in the number of active motor units"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An increase in the firing frequency to one motor unit"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A decrease in the number of active motor units"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because a whole muscle grades its force chiefly by recruiting additional motor units, each of which adds more all-or-none fibers to the contraction. OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology 2e states that as more strength is needed, larger motor units are enlisted, and that this increasing activation of motor units, known as recruitment, makes the muscle contraction grow progressively stronger (lines 17654-17657). Because a single motor neuron fires all the fibers in its motor unit together and those fibers respond all-or-none, the nervous system cannot make an individual fiber pull harder; instead it adds more motor units so that more fibers contract at once, which is why the same biceps can lift either a feather or a heavy weight (lines 17659-17660). (Choice A) is wrong because action potentials are all-or-none, so a stronger stimulus does not make a bigger action potential or a stronger contraction in a given fiber; all action potentials peak at the same voltage (lines 21981-21987). (Choice B) is correct: recruiting more motor units brings more all-or-none fibers online, which is the principal way a whole muscle increases its total force. (Choice C) describes wave summation, a real mechanism in which higher firing frequency to a motor unit raises its tension (lines 17730-17735), but it grades the force of fibers already active rather than adding new fibers, and the question highlights the all-or-none nature of fibers, which points specifically to recruiting additional motor units. (Choice D) reverses the actual relationship: as more motor units are recruited the contraction grows stronger, so producing more force requires increasing, not decreasing, the number of active motor units (lines 17656-17657). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that recruitment of additional motor units, not a larger response per fiber, is how a muscle grades its force.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
