-- Q11 [Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics', 'A physiologist stimulates an isolated skeletal muscle fiber and steadily raises the stimulus frequency until each new action potential arrives before the fiber can begin to relax, so the relaxation phase between contractions disappears entirely and tension remains continuous. This sustained response is best described as which state?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Incomplete tetanus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Motor unit recruitment"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete tetanus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Treppe (staircase effect)"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because when the stimulus frequency becomes so high that the relaxation phase disappears completely and contractions become continuous, the muscle is in complete (fused) tetanus. The textbook states, "If the stimulus frequency is so high that the relaxation phase disappears completely, contractions become continuous in a process called complete tetanus" (line 17748-17749), and that "During tetanus, the concentration of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions in the sarcoplasm allows virtually all of the sarcomeres to form cross-bridges and shorten, so that a contraction can continue uninterrupted" (line 17752-17753). The defining feature in the stem — total loss of the relaxation phase yielding continuous tension — is exactly what distinguishes complete tetanus. (Choice A) is the state reached as summation rises to a peak where "the muscle goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short relaxation phase for each" (line 17747-17748); because a brief relaxation phase still remains, incomplete tetanus does not match the stem''s full elimination of relaxation. (Choice B) refers to increasing "the number of motor units involved in contraction" (line 18358-18359), a separate mechanism for grading force by activating more fibers rather than by raising the firing frequency of one fiber, so it does not describe the continuous response produced here. (Choice C) is correct: raising frequency until the relaxation phase vanishes fuses the twitches into one uninterrupted contraction, which the text names complete tetanus. (Choice D) is the "stepwise increase in contraction tension" (line 18359) seen when a dormant muscle is first activated and successive twitches grow stronger while still returning to baseline between stimuli; treppe involves separate, fully relaxing twitches, not a fused continuous contraction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between stimulus frequency and the disappearance of the relaxation phase to identify the specific contractile state.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
