-- Q12 [Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics', 'A skeletal muscle fiber receives a second action potential from its motor neuron while it is still in the contraction phase of the first twitch, and the resulting twitch generates greater tension than the first. This increase in tension is most directly explained by which event at the molecular level?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "More \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) is released, activating additional sarcomeres"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Additional motor units are recruited to raise tension"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Warm-up makes successive contractions progressively more efficient"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The sarcoplasmic reticulum reabsorbs \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) more quickly between stimuli"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because a second stimulus arriving before the fiber relaxes triggers the release of more \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), which activates additional sarcomeres while the fiber is still contracting. This phenomenon is wave (frequency) summation, in which the excitation-contraction coupling effects of successive motor neuron signals add together. Because the first twitch has not yet relaxed, the new \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) adds to the cytosolic pool rather than replacing a depleted one, so more cross-bridges cycle simultaneously and tension rises. (Choice A) is correct: the added intracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) recruits sarcomeres that were not yet active, and as stimulus frequency climbs this summation builds toward incomplete and then complete tetanus. (Choice B) describes recruitment, the nervous system''s activation of additional, larger motor units to increase whole-muscle tension; that mechanism operates across many fibers and is not the molecular cause of a single fiber''s stronger second twitch at higher firing frequency. (Choice C) describes treppe, the staircase effect in which a previously dormant muscle''s early contractions are weaker and grow more efficient with steady stimulation; it is a distinct graded response and does not explain summation occurring within an unrelaxed twitch. (Choice D) reverses the relationship, because faster reuptake of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the sarcoplasmic reticulum drives relaxation and lowers tension, the opposite of the tension increase seen in summation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of excitation-contraction coupling to predict why a second, overlapping stimulus produces a stronger contraction.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
