-- Q14 [Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics', 'A physiologist records a myogram of a single skeletal muscle twitch and marks the latent period that precedes any measurable rise in tension. During this latent period, which of the following events are occurring in the muscle fiber?

I. Cross-bridges form between actin and myosin
II. The action potential propagates along the sarcolemma
III. \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the latent period is the interval when the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, while tension-generating cross-bridge formation has not yet begun. The OpenStax myogram description states that during the latent period "the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and Ca++ ions are released from the SR" and that this is "the phase during which excitation and contraction are being coupled but contraction has yet to occur." Cross-bridge formation is instead assigned to the subsequent contraction phase, where "cross-bridges have formed, and sarcomeres are actively shortening." Thus statements II and III describe events of the latent period, whereas statement I describes a later event. (Choice A) I only is wrong because cross-bridge formation belongs to the contraction phase, not the latent period; the text explicitly lists cross-bridge formation under "The contraction phase occurs next." (Choice B) III only is incomplete because action potential propagation along the sarcolemma (II) is also an explicit latent-period event, occurring alongside \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release. (Choice C) I and III only mistakenly includes cross-bridge formation (I), which has "yet to occur" during the latent period, while correctly including \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release (III). (Choice D) is correct: II and III only captures both latent-period events — sarcolemmal action potential propagation and SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release — that constitute excitation-contraction coupling before tension develops. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the timing of excitation-contraction coupling events to sort which processes precede measurable tension during the latent period.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
