-- Q15 [Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics] hard skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Mechanics & Contraction Dynamics', 'A physiologist records a myogram from an isolated skeletal muscle while increasing the stimulus frequency. For each frequency she notes the peak tension (relative to one twitch) and whether the trace returns to baseline between stimuli:

| Stimulus frequency (Hz) | Peak tension (× twitch) | Trace returns to baseline? |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1.0 | Yes, fully |
| 15 | 2.2 | Partially, with oscillations |
| 60 | 3.8 | No, smooth plateau |

At 60 Hz the muscle is most likely in which contractile state, and why?

A. Incomplete tetanus, because relaxation is briefly interrupted between stimuli
B. A single twitch, because the latent period has not yet elapsed
C. Complete tetanus, because sustained sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) keeps virtually all sarcomeres cross-bridged
D. Treppe, because warm-up raises sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) in a stepwise fashion', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Incomplete tetanus, because relaxation is briefly interrupted between stimuli"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A single twitch, because the latent period has not yet elapsed"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete tetanus, because sustained sarcoplasmic \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) keeps virtually all sarcomeres cross-bridged"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Treppe, because warm-up raises sarcoplasmic \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) in a stepwise fashion"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because at 60 Hz the trace shows a smooth plateau that never returns to baseline, the defining signature of complete tetanus. When stimulus frequency is so high that the relaxation phase disappears completely, contractions become continuous in a process called complete tetanus; during tetanus the concentration of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions in the sarcoplasm allows virtually all of the sarcomeres to form cross-bridges and shorten, so the contraction continues uninterrupted until the muscle fatigues. The continuously elevated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) arises because each successive stimulus triggers more \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release before reuptake can lower it, preventing tropomyosin from re-blocking the actin binding sites. The 60 Hz row''s "no, smooth plateau" therefore points to complete tetanus rather than any state in which tension oscillates. (Choice A) names incomplete tetanus, which is the 15 Hz row: there the muscle goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short relaxation phase for each, so the trace returns to baseline only partially and tension oscillates; at 60 Hz the relaxation phase has vanished, so it is no longer incomplete. (Choice B) describes a single twitch, the 1 Hz row, where each contraction fully relaxes; a single twitch produces no significant sustained work and cannot account for the maintained 3.8× plateau. (Choice C) is correct: only complete tetanus matches a continuous, non-oscillating plateau, and it is sustained \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) keeping nearly all sarcomeres cross-bridged that produces it. (Choice D) names treppe, the staircase effect seen when a dormant muscle is first activated and successive twitches grow stronger while still fully relaxing between stimuli; treppe traces still return to baseline, so it cannot explain a smooth plateau. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tension and baseline-return values in the myogram table to infer the contractile state and its calcium mechanism.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
