| Frequency (Hz) | Tension trace between peaks | Peak tension (× single twitch) |
|---|---|---|
| 5 | falls fully to baseline | 1.0 |
| 40 | dips partway, never to baseline | 3.5 |
| 80 | no dip, smooth plateau | 4.0 |', '[{"label": "A", "text": "40 Hz, reflecting incomplete tetanus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "80 Hz, reflecting complete tetanus"}, {"label": "C", "text": "5 Hz, reflecting wave summation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "5 Hz, reflecting treppe"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because incomplete tetanus is the state in which the muscle still goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short, partial relaxation phase between peaks even as summed tension keeps rising. As stimulus frequency increases, wave summation builds tension until it reaches a plateau about three to four times a single twitch; the textbook states that at this point, "During incomplete tetanus, the muscle goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short relaxation phase for each" (lines 17747-17748). The 40 Hz trace matches this signature exactly: tension dips partway between peaks (a partial, never-to-baseline relaxation) while peak tension climbs to 3.5× a single twitch, squarely within the predicted three-to-four-fold range. Only when frequency rises further does "the relaxation phase disappear completely," producing the continuous, dip-free contraction of complete tetanus (lines 17748-17750). (Choice A) is correct: the partial inter-peak relaxation combined with the ~3.5× summed tension is the defining fingerprint of incomplete (unfused) tetanus, where \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release is high enough to sustain elevated force but not yet high enough to abolish relaxation entirely. (Choice B) describes the 80 Hz trace, where the relaxation phase has vanished and the plateau is smooth; this is complete tetanus, which lacks the partial relaxation the stem asks about. (Choice C) names wave summation, but the 5 Hz trace falls fully to baseline between peaks, so successive twitches are not yet summing into a maintained elevated tension; summation requires a second stimulus arriving while the muscle is still contracting (lines 17730-17735). (Choice D) misattributes the 5 Hz trace to treppe, which is a graded staircase increase in force seen when a dormant muscle is first activated, not a partial relaxation between fused peaks (lines 17756-17761). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining criteria of incomplete versus complete tetanus to a tension recording and predict which frequency produces partial inter-peak relaxation.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 160)
