-- Q17 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'A sprinter explodes out of the blocks, and during the first few seconds of the all-out sprint the small pool of \(\text{ATP}\) stored in the leg muscles is nearly exhausted. Which mechanism most directly regenerates \(\text{ATP}\) to sustain the contraction during this initial period?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis converts glucose to pyruvic acid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Aerobic respiration breaks down glucose in mitochondria"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Creatine phosphate transfers its phosphate to ADP"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Myoglobin releases its stored reserve of oxygen"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because creatine phosphate donates its high-energy phosphate to \(\text{ADP}\) to form \(\text{ATP}\), and this is the source that powers the very first seconds of contraction. When a muscle begins to contract, creatine phosphate transfers its phosphate back to \(\text{ADP}\) to form \(\text{ATP}\) and creatine in a reaction catalyzed by creatine kinase that occurs very quickly; thus creatine phosphate-derived \(\text{ATP}\) powers the first few seconds of muscle contraction and can supply roughly 15 seconds of energy before another source is needed. (Choice A) Anaerobic glycolysis is a real ATP source in muscle, but the textbook states muscles turn to glycolysis only after the ATP produced by creatine phosphate is depleted, and glycolysis cannot generate ATP as quickly as creatine phosphate. (Choice B) Aerobic respiration in mitochondria supplies about 95 percent of the ATP for resting or moderately active muscle, but it is much slower and cannot meet the immediate demand of the first seconds of intense contraction. (Choice C) is correct: creatine phosphate acts as an energy reserve that quickly rephosphorylates \(\text{ADP}\) to \(\text{ATP}\) via creatine kinase, powering the opening seconds before glycolysis and aerobic respiration take over. (Choice D) Myoglobin stores a small reserve of oxygen to support aerobic contractions, but oxygen storage supports the slower aerobic pathway rather than directly regenerating \(\text{ATP}\) from \(\text{ADP}\). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that creatine phosphate rephosphorylates \(\text{ADP}\) to \(\text{ATP}\) during the first seconds of muscle contraction.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
