-- Q18 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'During an all-out sprint, a runner''s skeletal muscle cannot receive enough \(\text{O}_2\) to sustain aerobic respiration, and the pyruvic acid produced by glycolysis is instead converted to lactic acid. This conversion most directly allows continued ATP production because it accomplishes which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It generates the bulk of the cell''s ATP anaerobically"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It replenishes the muscle''s creatine phosphate reserve"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It oxidizes \\(\\text{NAD}^+\\) to \\(\\text{NADH}\\) for glycolysis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It regenerates \\(\\text{NAD}^+\\) from \\(\\text{NADH}\\) for glycolysis"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because reducing pyruvic acid to lactic acid reoxidizes \(\text{NADH}\) back to \(\text{NAD}^+\), and that regenerated \(\text{NAD}^+\) is the electron acceptor glycolysis needs to keep producing ATP when \(\text{O}_2\) is scarce. When oxygen cannot be delivered fast enough, pyruvic acid is converted to lactic acid, and the textbook states this "conversion allows the recycling of the enzyme NAD+ from NADH, which is needed for glycolysis to continue." The accompanying figure reinforces that "fermentation allows the reduction of pyruvate to lactate and the reoxidation of NADH to NAD+," and that "the regeneration of NAD+ allows glycolysis to continue to make ATP by substrate level phosphorylation." Thus lactic acid formation is not itself an ATP-yielding step; its functional payoff is restoring the oxidized cofactor so the ATP-generating reactions of glycolysis do not stall. (Choice A) is incorrect because the conversion of pyruvate to lactate yields no ATP, and glycolysis as a whole nets only "two ATPs per molecule of glucose," far from the bulk of cellular ATP, which normally comes from aerobic respiration in mitochondria. (Choice B) is incorrect because restoring creatine phosphate is a recovery process that the text says actually "requires oxygen" during repayment of the oxygen debt, the opposite of the oxygen-poor condition that drives lactic acid formation. (Choice C) is incorrect because it reverses the redox direction: lactic acid formation regenerates \(\text{NAD}^+\) by oxidizing \(\text{NADH}\), it does not oxidize \(\text{NAD}^+\) into \(\text{NADH}\); the figure specifies "the reoxidation of NADH to NAD+." (Choice D) is correct: regenerating \(\text{NAD}^+\) supplies glycolysis with the oxidized electron carrier it requires, sustaining substrate-level ATP production during the short window of anaerobic effort. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the role of \(\text{NAD}^+\) as a recycled electron carrier to explain why an apparent waste reaction sustains ATP output under low \(\text{O}_2\).', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
