-- Q19 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'After finishing an all-out sprint, a runner stops moving yet continues to breathe heavily for several minutes, taking in oxygen far above resting levels. This elevated post-exercise oxygen uptake most directly serves to:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "restore ATP and creatine phosphate and convert lactic acid back to pyruvic acid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "convert pyruvic acid into lactic acid to regenerate \\(\\text{NAD}^+\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "pump \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum to end contraction"}, {"label": "D", "text": "saturate myoglobin so that anaerobic glycolysis can begin"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the excess oxygen taken in after intense exercise (the oxygen debt) is used to rebuild depleted \(\text{ATP}\) and creatine phosphate stores and to convert accumulated lactic acid back to pyruvic acid. During an all-out sprint, muscle relies heavily on anaerobic glycolysis, depleting \(\text{ATP}\) and creatine phosphate and producing lactic acid that lowers intracellular pH. The textbook states that oxygen debt is "the amount of oxygen needed to compensate for ATP produced without oxygen during muscle contraction" and that "oxygen is required to restore ATP and creatine phosphate levels, convert lactic acid to pyruvic acid, and, in the liver, to convert lactic acid into glucose or glycogen," which is why oxygen intake stays elevated until the debt is met. (Choice A) is correct: it names the actual recovery processes—replenishing the rapid-energy stores (\(\text{ATP}\) and creatine phosphate) and reversing the lactic-acid accumulation—that the post-exercise oxygen pays back. (Choice B) reverses the relevant direction; converting pyruvic acid to lactic acid (which regenerates \(\text{NAD}^+\)) occurs during anaerobic exercise when oxygen is scarce, whereas recovery converts lactic acid back to pyruvic acid. (Choice C) describes relaxation, in which ATP-driven pumps move \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR; this happens after each contraction and is not the function of the post-exercise oxygen debt. (Choice D) misassigns myoglobin''s role: myoglobin stores a small oxygen reserve for aerobic respiration, and glycolysis is anaerobic, so saturating myoglobin does not initiate glycolysis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of oxygen debt to a specific post-exercise scenario and identify which recovery processes the excess oxygen supports.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
