-- Q20 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] medium skill4 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'A physiologist measures the net \(\text{ATP}\) yield per glucose molecule for the pathways a skeletal muscle fiber uses to regenerate \(\text{ATP}\):

| Pathway | Net ATP per glucose | \(\text{O}_2\) required |
|---|---|---|
| Anaerobic glycolysis | 2 | No |
| Lactic acid fermentation | 2 | No |
| Aerobic respiration | 36 | Yes |

Based on the table, the pathway that most efficiently extracts \(\text{ATP}\) from each glucose molecule is the one that most directly depends on which feature?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Rapid regeneration of \\(\\text{NAD}^+\\) in the cytosol"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oxidation of glucose within the mitochondria"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Phosphate transfer from creatine phosphate"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Conversion of pyruvic acid into lactic acid"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the pathway with the highest yield in the table, aerobic respiration at 36 \(\text{ATP}\) per glucose, is the one that oxidizes glucose in the mitochondria using \(\text{O}_2\). The table shows aerobic respiration producing approximately 36 \(\text{ATP}\) per glucose versus only 2 for the anaerobic routes, and the textbook states that aerobic respiration is the breakdown of glucose in the presence of \(\text{O}_2\) to produce carbon dioxide, water, and \(\text{ATP}\), and that this process takes place in mitochondria (lines 17502-17504). Because this pathway is much more efficient than anaerobic glycolysis, its high yield depends directly on \(\text{O}_2\)-dependent oxidation inside the mitochondria, which the table flags as the only \(\text{O}_2\)-requiring pathway. (Choice A) Regeneration of \(\text{NAD}^+\) is what lactic acid production accomplishes to allow glycolysis to continue, but this only sustains the low-yield 2-\(\text{ATP}\) anaerobic route and does not raise efficiency (lines 17493-17499). (Choice B) is correct: the 36-\(\text{ATP}\) yield is generated only when pyruvic acid is fully oxidized in the mitochondria in the presence of \(\text{O}_2\), the feature unique to the highest-yield row. (Choice C) Creatine phosphate transfers a phosphate to ADP to power the first few seconds of contraction, but it does not break down glucose and so contributes no per-glucose yield in the table (lines 17467-17470). (Choice D) Converting pyruvic acid to lactic acid occurs when \(\text{O}_2\) is absent and yields no additional \(\text{ATP}\) beyond the 2 from glycolysis, so it represents a low-efficiency, not high-efficiency, route (lines 17498-17500). This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated \(\text{ATP}\) yields and \(\text{O}_2\) requirements to connect the highest-efficiency pathway to its underlying mitochondrial mechanism.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
