-- Q21 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'After a sprinter crosses the finish line, her breathing rate stays elevated while her body repays the oxygen debt incurred during the race. Which of the following processes consume the oxygen taken up during this recovery period?

I. Anaerobic glycolysis regenerating \(\text{ATP}\) within the working muscle fibers
II. Restoration of creatine phosphate stores in the muscle
III. Hepatic conversion of lactic acid into glucose', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because repaying the oxygen debt uses oxygen to rebuild creatine phosphate stores and to let the liver reconvert lactic acid into glucose, whereas anaerobic glycolysis itself uses no oxygen. The oxygen debt is the amount of oxygen needed to compensate for \(\text{ATP}\) produced without oxygen during contraction, and oxygen is required to restore \(\text{ATP}\) and creatine phosphate levels, convert lactic acid to pyruvic acid, and, in the liver, convert lactic acid into glucose or glycogen (lines 17529-17531). Statement II (creatine phosphate restoration) and Statement III (hepatic conversion of lactic acid to glucose) are both explicitly named recovery processes powered by post-exercise oxygen uptake, which is why oxygen intake stays elevated after exercise stops. Statement I describes the anaerobic process that created the debt rather than a process that repays it. (Choice A) is wrong because it selects only Statement I; anaerobic glycolysis is a "non-oxygen-dependent" process (line 17486) that occurs during exertion when oxygen cannot be sufficiently delivered (lines 17495-17496), so it consumes no oxygen and contributes to the debt rather than repaying it. (Choice B) is a partial answer that captures only the hepatic process and omits creatine phosphate restoration, which is equally an oxygen-consuming recovery process (line 17530). (Choice C) correctly includes hepatic lactic-acid conversion (III) but is wrong because it adds anaerobic glycolysis (I), conflating the anaerobic process that generates the debt with the aerobic processes that repay it. (Choice D) is correct: restoring creatine phosphate (II) and the liver''s conversion of lactic acid to glucose (III) are both oxygen-requiring recovery steps named in the oxygen-debt passage, while anaerobic glycolysis is excluded because it produces \(\text{ATP}\) without oxygen. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of oxygen debt to sort candidate processes by whether each occurs aerobically during recovery or anaerobically during exertion.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
