-- Q22 [Muscle Energetics & Fatigue] hard skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Muscle Energetics & Fatigue', 'A physiologist measures how long a single skeletal muscle can sustain each of three efforts before its ATP source must change:

| Effort | Duration before ATP source switches |
|---|---|
| Maximal jump-and-sprint burst | ~12 s |
| Repeated high-intensity intervals | ~55 s |
| Steady distance jog | minutes to hours |

For the maximal jump-and-sprint burst, the ATP that powers the muscle is most directly regenerated by which mechanism?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aerobic respiration in the mitochondria"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis of stored glycogen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Transfer of phosphate from creatine phosphate"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oxygen release from myoglobin stores"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because a burst lasting only about 12 seconds falls within the roughly 15-second window powered by creatine phosphate transferring its phosphate to \(\text{ADP}\). The textbook states that stored \(\text{ATP}\) powers only a few seconds, after which "creatine phosphate transfers its phosphate back to ADP to form ATP and creatine," a reaction "catalyzed by the enzyme creatine kinase" that "occurs very quickly; thus, creatine phosphate-derived ATP powers the first few seconds of muscle contraction" but "can only provide approximately 15 seconds worth of energy." The ~12 s effort in the table is shorter than this 15 s ceiling and shorter than the ~55 s and minutes-to-hours efforts, so it is supplied before the muscle switches to the slower glycolytic and aerobic systems. The duration data therefore map this maximal burst onto the creatine phosphate phase of the energy timeline. (Choice A) Aerobic respiration provides "approximately 95 percent of the ATP required for resting or moderately active muscles" and "cannot be sustained without a steady supply of O2," matching the minutes-to-hours steady jog, not a 12 s burst. (Choice B) Anaerobic glycolysis takes over only after creatine phosphate is depleted and "cannot be sustained for very long (approximately 1 minute of muscle activity)," matching the ~55 s interval effort rather than the brief burst. (Choice C) is correct: at ~12 s the muscle is still inside the ~15 s creatine phosphate window, where creatine kinase rapidly rephosphorylates \(\text{ADP}\) to \(\text{ATP}\). (Choice D) Myoglobin stores \(\text{O}_2\) for aerobic respiration and is an oxygen reserve, not a direct \(\text{ATP}\)-regenerating reaction, so it cannot itself regenerate the \(\text{ATP}\) consumed during the burst. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated effort durations and match the ~12 s burst to the correct phase of the ATP-source timeline.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
