-- Q24 [Cardiac Muscle Function] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cardiac Muscle Function', 'During a carotid sinus massage, a clinician increases vagal output to the heart, and the patient''s resting heart rate falls. This slowing is most directly caused by the released neurotransmitter acting on the pacemaker cells of the SA node in which way?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Norepinephrine binds adrenergic receptors and depolarizes the cells faster"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Acetylcholine opens calcium channels to trigger a stronger contraction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Acetylcholine closes potassium channels and depolarizes the cells faster"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acetylcholine binds muscarinic receptors and hyperpolarizes the cells"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because parasympathetic (vagal) fibers release acetylcholine, which binds muscarinic receptors on pacemaker cells and hyperpolarizes them, slowing the rate of spontaneous depolarization and lowering heart rate. The textbook states that at the regions of the heart that establish heart rate, "If ACh is released onto those cells, it binds to a muscarinic receptor that causes the cells to hyperpolarize so that they cannot reach threshold as easily, and the heart rate slows" (lines 28110-28112). Mechanistically, ACh "slows HR by opening chemical- or ligand-gated potassium ion channels to slow the rate of spontaneous depolarization, which extends repolarization and increases the time before the next spontaneous depolarization occurs" (lines 37290-37292); the resulting \(\text{K}^+\) efflux drives the membrane more negative, making it harder for the pacemaker cells to reach threshold. (Choice A) describes the opposite, sympathetic effect: norepinephrine binds adrenergic receptors and "causes the cells to depolarize faster, and the heart rate increases" (lines 28109-28110), which would speed the heart rather than slow it. (Choice B) confuses pacemaker rate control with excitation-contraction coupling; ACh acting on pacemaker cells modulates the timing of spontaneous depolarization, not \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) influx that triggers a stronger contraction, and parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart rather than strengthening contraction. (Choice C) reverses the actual channel behavior; ACh opens (not closes) potassium channels, producing \(\text{K}^+\) efflux that hyperpolarizes the cell, so closing those channels and depolarizing faster contradicts the mechanism by which ACh slows HR (lines 37290-37292). (Choice D) is correct: muscarinic binding of ACh hyperpolarizes the SA-node pacemaker cells, lengthening the interval before the next spontaneous depolarization and thereby decreasing heart rate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the parasympathetic ACh-muscarinic mechanism to predict the membrane response of pacemaker cells in a specific clinical maneuver.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
