-- Q25 [Cardiac Muscle Function] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cardiac Muscle Function', 'A physiologist stimulates an isolated strip of skeletal muscle at high frequency and produces a smooth, sustained (tetanic) contraction, but the same high-frequency stimulation applied to a strip of cardiac muscle fails to produce a sustained contraction. Which of the following statements help explain why cardiac muscle CANNOT be tetanized?

I. Skeletal muscle fibers lack a refractory period during a twitch contraction.
II. In cardiac muscle, the refractory period lasts nearly as long as the contraction itself.
III. The plateau phase of the cardiac action potential prolongs the absolute refractory period.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized since its refractory period is prolonged by the action-potential plateau and lasts nearly as long as the contraction, leaving no window for a second stimulus to summate. In skeletal muscle, the action potential and its refractory period are extremely brief (the whole action potential takes roughly 2 ms), so a fiber that is still in the relaxation phase of a twitch can be re-stimulated; rapid stimulation makes successive twitches fuse into wave summation and, at high enough frequency, complete tetanus. In cardiac contractile cells the slow \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels produce a long plateau phase, giving an absolute refractory period of about 200 ms and a total refractory period near 250 ms — almost the full duration of the mechanical contraction — so the cell has finished or nearly finished contracting before it can be excited again, which prevents the summation needed for tetany. (Choice A) I only is wrong because statement I is false. (Choice B) III only is wrong because it omits the true statement II. (Choice C) I and III only is wrong because it includes the false statement I and omits the true statement II. (Choice D) is correct: statements II and III are both true and together explain the prohibition on cardiac tetanus — the plateau phase (III) lengthens the absolute refractory period so that the refractory period spans nearly the entire contraction (II), eliminating any interval in which a second action potential could summate; statement I is false because skeletal fibers do have a refractory period, but it is brief (about 2 ms) and ends long before the twitch''s mechanical relaxation, which is precisely what allows skeletal summation and tetanus. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between action-potential timing and contraction timing to two different muscle types to explain why one can be tetanized and the other cannot.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
